1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,045
Lesson 1.3.

2
00:00:02,425 --> 00:00:04,937
Shannon entropy and Gibbs entropy.

3
00:00:06,050 --> 00:00:10,961
Shannon, thinking about telegraph
messages, wrote a formula for information.

4
00:00:11,341 --> 00:00:14,198
Gibbs, seventy years
earlier, had written the

5
00:00:14,198 --> 00:00:16,611
same formula for the entropy of a gas.

6
00:00:16,991 --> 00:00:18,237
Is it a coincidence?

7
00:00:18,617 --> 00:00:19,504
Not at all.

8
00:00:19,884 --> 00:00:23,801
In this lesson we will see that
both measure the same thing,

9
00:00:23,801 --> 00:00:28,044
uncertainty about a state, and
that the constant converting them,

10
00:00:28,044 --> 00:00:31,895
k_B ln 2, is the bridge between
bits and joules per kelvin.

11
00:00:32,275 --> 00:00:35,786
That is Landauer's phrase:
information is physical.

12
00:00:36,917 --> 00:00:37,973
Let's play a guessing game.

13
00:00:38,353 --> 00:00:41,000
I hide one card among
eight equally likely ones.

14
00:00:41,380 --> 00:00:46,184
The best strategy is to split the set
in half with each yes-or-no question.

15
00:00:46,564 --> 00:00:49,648
With three questions I
always find it: three bits.

16
00:00:50,028 --> 00:00:54,171
Now suppose the cards are not
equally likely: one comes up half

17
00:00:54,171 --> 00:00:57,657
the time, another a quarter,
and two one eighth each.

18
00:00:58,037 --> 00:01:00,581
It pays to ask about the
most likely one first.

19
00:01:00,961 --> 00:01:04,520
The average number of
questions drops to 1.75.

20
00:01:04,900 --> 00:01:08,780
That figure is exactly the Shannon
entropy of the distribution.

21
00:01:09,883 --> 00:01:15,274
Shannon entropy is H = −Σ p log₂ p.

22
00:01:15,654 --> 00:01:19,579
Each outcome of probability
p contributes a surprise of

23
00:01:19,579 --> 00:01:23,431
minus log p bits, and entropy
is the average surprise.

24
00:01:23,811 --> 00:01:29,157
Shannon's coding theorem says no
code can use, on average, fewer than

25
00:01:29,157 --> 00:01:33,882
H bits per symbol, and that you
can get as close as you like.

26
00:01:34,262 --> 00:01:38,350
In the question tree, the depth
of each leaf is its surprise.

27
00:01:39,483 --> 00:01:46,106
Gibbs entropy applies the same
formula to physical microstates, with

28
00:01:46,106 --> 00:01:51,949
the natural log and Boltzmann's
constant: S = −k_B Σ p ln p.

29
00:01:52,329 --> 00:01:56,557
If all accessible microstates
are equally likely,

30
00:01:56,557 --> 00:02:00,352
it reduces to k_B ln
Ω, Boltzmann's formula.

31
00:02:00,732 --> 00:02:03,387
And if the distribution
is Boltzmann's, Gibbs

32
00:02:03,387 --> 00:02:05,628
entropy matches the thermodynamic one.

33
00:02:06,008 --> 00:02:10,212
Changing the log base is
just changing units: one

34
00:02:10,212 --> 00:02:13,814
bit is worth k_B ln 2 of physical entropy.

35
00:02:14,917 --> 00:02:19,466
A memory is a physical system whose
space of microstates is divided

36
00:02:19,466 --> 00:02:23,336
into logical regions: the zero
region and the one region.

37
00:02:23,716 --> 00:02:30,434
The total physical entropy splits into two
parts: the Shannon entropy of the logical

38
00:02:30,434 --> 00:02:36,273
states, times k_B ln 2, plus the
average internal entropy of each region.

39
00:02:36,654 --> 00:02:41,297
When we erase, we merge the logical
regions: the logical part disappears and,

40
00:02:41,297 --> 00:02:45,338
by the second law, that entropy
has to go out into the environment.

41
00:02:45,718 --> 00:02:48,983
Bits are not abstractions:
they count microstates.

42
00:02:50,083 --> 00:02:53,363
A gigabyte of completely
random data contains

43
00:02:53,363 --> 00:02:56,132
eight billion bits of Shannon entropy.

44
00:02:56,512 --> 00:03:01,369
As physical entropy, that is eight billion

45
00:03:01,369 --> 00:03:05,763
times k_B ln 2: about 7.7 × 10⁻¹⁴ J/K.

46
00:03:06,143 --> 00:03:08,506
It is tiny compared
with the thermal entropy

47
00:03:08,506 --> 00:03:10,600
of the chip itself, but it is not zero.

48
00:03:10,980 --> 00:03:14,269
Erasing it at room temperature requires

49
00:03:14,269 --> 00:03:17,725
expelling at least 2.3 × 10⁻¹¹ J of heat.

50
00:03:18,105 --> 00:03:21,183
If the data were text
compressible to two bits per

51
00:03:21,183 --> 00:03:24,753
character, the minimum cost
would be proportionally lower.

52
00:03:25,883 --> 00:03:34,033
In practice, one bit is worth k_B ln
2, about 9.6 × 10⁻²⁴ joules per kelvin.

53
00:03:34,413 --> 00:03:38,007
Data compression sets how
many bits must be stored and,

54
00:03:38,007 --> 00:03:41,013
by Landauer, the minimum
cost of erasing them.

55
00:03:41,393 --> 00:03:44,542
And when a thermodynamic
chip produces a sample, the

56
00:03:44,542 --> 00:03:47,812
randomness it contains is
borrowed from the heat bath.

57
00:03:48,917 --> 00:03:53,276
In the next lesson Maxwell's demon
will turn information into work.

58
00:03:53,656 --> 00:03:55,722
Then we will see the cost of erasing it.

59
00:03:56,102 --> 00:04:00,522
In unit three entropy will become
a trajectory-level quantity, and in

60
00:04:00,522 --> 00:04:04,622
unit eight the KL divergence will
give the geometry of learning.

61
00:04:05,750 --> 00:04:08,547
Common mistakes: mixing bits with nats.

62
00:04:08,927 --> 00:04:11,912
Thinking one entropy is
subjective and the other

63
00:04:11,912 --> 00:04:15,083
objective, when both depend
on how we group states.

64
00:04:15,463 --> 00:04:17,745
Believing meaningful text has more entropy

65
00:04:17,745 --> 00:04:20,135
than noise, when it is
the other way around.

66
00:04:20,515 --> 00:04:23,688
And forgetting the internal
entropy of each logical state.

67
00:04:24,817 --> 00:04:28,121
In summary: Shannon entropy
is the average number

68
00:04:28,121 --> 00:04:30,954
of binary questions, the average surprise.

69
00:04:31,334 --> 00:04:34,853
Gibbs entropy is the same formula
over physical microstates.

70
00:04:35,233 --> 00:04:40,048
One bit is worth k_B ln 2 of
physical entropy, and the entropy of

71
00:04:40,048 --> 00:04:44,197
a memory splits into a logical
part and an internal one.

72
00:04:44,577 --> 00:04:46,119
Information is physical.

73
00:04:47,250 --> 00:04:50,893
To practice: compute an
entropy and its optimal code.

74
00:04:51,273 --> 00:04:54,861
Count the questions needed to guess
a number between one and a thousand.

75
00:04:55,241 --> 00:04:58,176
Convert one nat to bits
and to joules per kelvin.

76
00:04:58,556 --> 00:05:01,576
And compute the logical
entropy of a biased memory.
